even before the meeting got started the  press were allowed in for a quick look  all right thank you folks the white  house signaled they were open to hearing  from republicans  but not necessarily reducing the amount  president biden wants to spend  it's important to him that he hears this  group out  on their concerns on their ideas he's  always open to making  this package stronger and the democrats  in the house and senate didn't wait for  the oval office sit down to move forward  with their one point trillion dollar  bill  putting in motion the process to try and  pass it without any support from the  opposition party  republican senators are hoping to spend  just 600 billion dollars  the two-hour meeting didn't appear to  produce any breakthroughs  i wouldn't say that we came together  on a package tonight no one expected  that  in a two-hour meeting but what we did  agreed to do  is to follow up and talk further  the republican plan would spend money on  fighting covid give some financial  supports the lowest earning americans  and the unemployed  and some money for schools to reopen but  it left out a key democratic demand  billions for states and cities who were  facing budget shortfalls and may have to  fire employees  like teachers firefighters garbage  collectors  it also didn't include raising the  federal minimum wage to 15 dollars an  hour over five years  president biden has said the need is  urgent and he promised voters that help  would quickly be on the way  but he also pledged to find compromise  in congress  now as they continue to negotiate the  question is  how long is he willing to wait to see if  he can fulfill  both big promises patti culhane al  jazeera  washington
 

